                <pb facs="0479" n="394" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg394"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">815.00/2102: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul in Charge of the
                        Legation in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lawton</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-11-12T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 12,
                                1919, 5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>You may inform President Bogran that the Department regards the executive power
                    as being in his charge in accordance with the terms of the constitution of
                    Honduras. (Article 106)</p>
                <p>Your October 30, 8 p.m. Department believes that it is most advisable that
                    executive power should remain in charge of Bogran until the expiration of the
                    present term.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
